et.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2\u003eAt-a-Glance Comparison Table\u003c/h2\u003e\u003ctable\u003e\u003cthead\u003e\u003ctr\u003e\u003cth\u003eCategory\u003c/th\u003e\u003cth\u003eDolphin\u003c/th\u003e\u003cth\u003ePolaris\u003c/th\u003e\u003c/tr\u003e\u003c/thead\u003e\u003ctbody\u003e\u003ctr\u003e\u003ctd\u003eBest For\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eMost residential pool owners\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eFeature-focused buyers\u003c/td\u003e\u003c/tr\u003e\u003ctr\u003e\u003ctd\u003eCleaning Performance\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eConsistently strong floor and wall cleaning\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eStrong cleaning with premium mobility on select models\u003c/td\u003e\u003c/tr\u003e\u003ctr\u003e\u003ctd\u003eNavigation\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eEfficient model-dependent navigation\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eAdvanced navigation and app features on higher-end models\u003c/td\u003e\u003c/tr\u003e\u003ctr\u003e\u003ctd\u003eFiltration\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eSimple baskets or flexible multi-filter systems\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eLarge canisters and strong debris handling\u003c/td\u003e\u003c/tr\u003e\u003ctr\u003e\u003ctd\u003eEase of Use\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eUsually simpler and more straightforward\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eMore features, sometimes more setup\u003c/td\u003e\u003c/tr\u003e\u003ctr\u003e\u003ctd\u003eValue\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eExcellent in the mid-range\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eBest value at the premium end\u003c/td\u003e\u003c/tr\u003e\u003ctr\u003e\u003ctd\u003eTypical Buyer\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eWants dependable cleaning without overpaying\u003c/td\u003e\u003ctd\u003eWants smart controls and premium build quality\u003c/td\u003e\u003c/tr\u003e\u003c/tbody\u003e\u003c/table\u003e\u003ch2\u003eCleaning Performance\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp\u003eCleaning performance is the main reason to buy a robotic pool cleaner, and both Polaris and Dolphin make models capable of excellent results. The difference is less about whether either brand can clean a pool and more about how each brand approaches the job.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eDolphin tends to focus on efficient scrubbing, reliable wall climbing, and practical coverage. Many Dolphin models use active brushes that help loosen dirt, algae film, and fine debris from pool surfaces before the robot vacuums it into the filter basket or cartridge. This makes Dolphin especially appealing for owners who want a cleaner that handles the basic jobs well without requiring much thought.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePolaris robotic cleaners often emphasize power, traction, and premium movement systems. Higher-end Polaris models can feel more substantial, with strong vacuum action and confident movement around larger or more complex pools. In particular, the Polaris 9650iQ Sport uses a four-wheel-drive design that can help it move over drains, slopes, and irregular surfaces more confidently than some lighter robots.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eFor typical in-ground pools, Dolphin usually provides the better balance of cleaning ability and price. For complex pools with unusual shapes, raised features, or owners who want a more advanced machine, Polaris becomes more competitive.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2\u003eNavigation and Smart Features\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp\u003eNavigation is one of the biggest areas where model selection matters. Neither brand should be judged by one robot alone. Entry-level and mid-range cleaners from both brands will behave differently from premium models.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eDolphin cleaners generally prioritize efficient automated coverage. Some models use scanning or route-planning systems, while simpler models rely on proven movement patterns that are still effective for many residential pools. The practical benefit is that Dolphin often feels easy to live with. You place the robot in the pool, start a cycle, and let it work.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePolaris has a stronger identity around premium smart features on select models. App control, cleaning modes, lift systems, and status information can make ownership feel more modern. These features can be useful if you want remote control, scheduling flexibility, or the ability to guide the cleaner toward a problem area.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe tradeoff is that smart features do not automatically mean better cleaning. A simpler Dolphin can still be the better purchase if it cleans your pool reliably and costs less. A premium Polaris makes more sense when the smart features solve a real ownership problem rather than simply adding novelty.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2\u003eFiltration and Debris Handling\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp\u003eFiltration is where buyers should think carefully about their pool environment. A pool surrounded by screens or open patio space has different needs than a pool under trees.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eDolphin offers a wide range of filtration approaches. Some models keep things simple with easy-access baskets or cartridges. Others, such as the Dolphin Premier, provide more filtration flexibility for different debris types. That flexibility can matter if your pool gets fine dust at one time of year and large leaves at another.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePolaris models often use large debris canisters that are easy to remove and rinse. This can be useful for pools that collect leaves, acorns, seed pods, and other larger debris. Polaris also tends to perform well for buyers who want a sturdy cleaner that feels built for heavier cleanup jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eFor fine debris and everyday cleaning, Dolphin is often the stronger value. For large debris and premium canister convenience, Polaris can be very appealing. If heavy debris is your main concern, compare specific models rather than choosing by brand alone.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2\u003eEase of Ownership\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe best robotic pool cleaner is not just the one that cleans well. It also needs to be easy enough that you will actually use it regularly.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eDolphin has an advantage here because many of its most popular models are intentionally straightforward. The controls are simple, filter access is usually convenient, and the learning curve is low. This is one reason Dolphin models appear frequently in buyer guides such as our \u003ca href=\"/best-of/overall\"\u003ebest robotic pool cleaners\u003c/a\u003e list.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePolaris ownership can feel more premium, but it may also involve more features to understand. App setup, cleaning modes, lift systems, and additional controls can be helpful, but they add a layer of interaction. Some owners love that. Others would rather press one button and be done.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eWeight and handling also matter. Robotic cleaners are often heavier when full of water. Polaris models with robust construction may feel more substantial, while some Dolphin models are easier to manage depending on the exact unit. Before buying, consider who will lift the cleaner out of the pool, where it will be stored, and how often it will run.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2\u003eLong-Term Value\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp\u003eLong-term value is where Dolphin usually has the edge. The brand offers many models that deliver strong performance without requiring a premium budget. Replacement filters and common parts are widely available, and the large owner base makes it easier to find guidance on maintenance and troubleshooting.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePolaris can still be a good long-term value, but the equation is different. You are often paying more upfront for build quality, smart features, and premium convenience. That can be worthwhile if you use those features regularly or if your pool benefits from the brand's mobility and debris-handling strengths.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eBuyers focused on price-to-performance should start with Dolphin and compare models in our \u003ca href=\"/best-of/value\"\u003ebest value robotic pool cleaners\u003c/a\u003e guide. Buyers focused on premium features should look closely at Polaris, especially if app control and easier retrieval matter.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAlso consider corded versus cordless design before making a final decision. Most Polaris and Dolphin robotic cleaners in this comparison are corded, which generally provides more consistent runtime and power. For a broader explanation, see our guide to \u003ca href=\"/blog/corded-vs-cordless-robotic-pool-cleaners\"\u003ecorded vs cordless robotic pool cleaners\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2\u003eWho Should Choose Dolphin\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp\u003eChoose Dolphin if you want the safer recommendation for most residential pools. The brand is especially strong for buyers who care about value, predictable cleaning, and simple maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cul\u003e\u003cli\u003eYou want strong cleaning without paying for premium smart features.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eYou prefer simple controls and a low learning curve.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eYour pool has normal debris such as dirt, bugs, pollen, and occasional leaves.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eYou want a wide selection of models at different price points.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eYou are comparing proven mid-range options like the Nautilus CC Plus.\u003c/li\u003e\u003c/ul\u003e\u003cp\u003eDolphin is also a strong choice if you are unsure what you need. Its popular models tend to cover the needs of the average pool owner well.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2\u003eWho Should Choose Polaris\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp\u003eChoose Polaris if you want a more premium robotic cleaner and are comfortable paying for added features. Polaris is often most compelling for buyers who want app control, strong mobility, and a more feature-rich ownership experience.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cul\u003e\u003cli\u003eYou want smart controls and scheduling features.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eYour pool has a complex shape, drains, slopes, or obstacles.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eYou value rugged construction and premium handling.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eYou regularly deal with larger debris.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003eYou are considering higher-end models such as the Polaris 9650iQ Sport.\u003c/li\u003e\u003c/ul\u003e\u003cp\u003ePolaris is not necessarily the budget choice, but it can be the right choice when its features match your pool and your expectations.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2\u003eFinal Recommendation\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp\u003eIf you want the best brand for most pool owners in 2026, choose \u003cstrong\u003eDolphin\u003c/strong\u003e. It offers the stronger overall combination of cleaning performance, model selection, ease of use, and value. The Dolphin Nautilus CC Plus is a practical starting point for many buyers, while the Dolphin Premier makes sense for pools with more demanding filtration needs.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIf you want a more premium experience and are willing to pay for it, choose \u003cstrong\u003ePolaris\u003c/strong\u003e. The brand's higher-end robotic cleaners offer smart features, confident mobility, and a more advanced feel that some pool owners will appreciate.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe most useful comparison is often model to model rather than brand to brand. If you are deciding between two specific cleaners, read our direct comparison of the \u003ca href=\"/blog/dolphin-nautilus-cc-plus-vs-polaris-9650iq-sport\"\u003eDolphin Nautilus CC Plus vs Polaris 9650iQ Sport\u003c/a\u003e for a closer look at how a value-focused Dolphin stacks up against a premium Polaris.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2\u003eRelated Resources\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cul\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"/reviews/Dolphin-Nautilus-CC-Plus\"\u003eDolphin Nautilus CC Plus Review\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"/reviews/Dolphin-Premier\"\u003eDolphin Premier Review\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"/reviews/Polaris-9650iQ-Sport\"\u003ePolaris 9650iQ Sport Review\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"/best-of/overall\"\u003eBest Robotic Pool Cleaners\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"/best-of/value\"\u003eBest Value Robotic Pool Cleaners\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"/blog/dolphin-nautilus-cc-plus-vs-polaris-9650iq-sport\"\u003eDolphin Nautilus CC Plus vs Polaris 9650iQ Sport\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"/blog/corded-vs-cordless-robotic-pool-cleaners\"\u003eCorded vs Cordless Robotic Pool Cleaners\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\u003c/ul\u003e\u003ch2\u003eFrequently Asked Questions\u003c/h2\u003e\u003ch3\u003eIs Dolphin better than Polaris?\u003c/h3\u003e\u003cp\u003eDolphin is better for most buyers because it generally offers stronger value, simpler ownership, and a broader range of practical models. Polaris can be better for buyers who want premium smart features, rugged mobility, and app-based control.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch3\u003eIs Polaris worth the extra money?\u003c/h3\u003e\u003cp\u003ePolaris can be worth the extra money if you will use its premium features or if your pool benefits from stronger mobility and large debris handling. If you only need routine floor and wall cleaning, a Dolphin may be the better value.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch3\u003eWhich brand is better for leaves?\u003c/h3\u003e\u003cp\u003eBoth brands offer models that can handle leaves, but the best choice depends on the specific cleaner. Polaris can be strong for larger debris canisters, while Dolphin Premier-style filtration can be useful for mixed debris conditions.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch3\u003eWhich brand is easier to use?\u003c/h3\u003e\u003cp\u003eDolphin is usually easier for owners who want simple operation. Polaris may offer more controls and connected features, which can be helpful but may also add setup steps.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch3\u003eShould I compare brands or individual models?\u003c/h3\u003e\u003cp\u003eUse the brand comparison as a starting point, but make the final decision by comparing individual models. Features, cable length, filtration style, weight, and cleaning modes vary significantly within each brand.\u003c/p\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3:[[[\"$\",\"link\",\"0\",{\"rel\":\"stylesheet\",\"href\":\"/_next/static/css/d071dca61fbb01bf.css\",\"precedence\":\"next\",\"crossOrigin\":\"\"}],[\"$\",\"link\",\"1\",{\"rel\":\"stylesheet\",\"href\":\"/_next/static/css/0fc6c400c3ed5371.css\",\"precedence\":\"next\",\"crossOrigin\":\"\"}]],[\"$\",\"$L4\",null,{\"buildId\":\"YtuVVRAWbOvBWnkgNkluo\",\"assetPrefix\":\"\",\"initialCanonicalUrl\":\"/blog/polaris-vs-dolphin-robotic-pool-cleaners\",\"initialTree\":[\"\",{\"children\":[\"blog\",{\"children\":[[\"slug\",\"polaris-vs-dolphin-robotic-pool-cleaners\",\"d\"],{\"children\":[\"__PAGE__?{\\\"slug\\\":\\\"polaris-vs-dolphin-robotic-pool-cleaners\\\"}\",{}]}]}]},\"$undefined\",\"$undefined\",true],\"initialHead\":[false,\"$L5\"],\"globalErrorComponent\":\"$6\",\"children\":[null,[\"$\",\"html\",null,{\"lang\":\"en\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"body\",null,{\"className\":\"d-flex flex-column min-vh-100\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"nav\",null,{\"className\":\"navbar navbar-expand-lg navbar-light bg-light\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"container\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L7\",null,{\"href\":\"/\",\"className\":\"navbar-brand d-flex align-items-center\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"style\":{\"width\":\"160px\",\"height\":\"90px\",\"position\":\"relative\",\"marginRight\":\"10px\"},\"children\":[\"$\",\"img\",null,{\"src\":\"/images/ThePoolLab.png\",\"alt\":\"The Pool Lab Logo\",\"style\":{\"width\":\"100%\",\"height\":\"100%\",\"objectFit\":\"contain\"}}]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"d-none d-sm-inline\",\"children\":\"The Pool Lab\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"button\",null,{\"className\":\"navbar-toggler\",\"type\":\"button\",\"data-bs-toggle\":\"collapse\",\"data-bs-target\":\"#navbarNav\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"navbar-toggler-icon\"}]}],[\"$\",\"d